      "content": "The distinction lies in a combination of both grammatical correctness and stylistic conventions in formal writing.\n\nColloquialisms, such as \"i been tryin\" or \"gonna,\" often deviate from standard grammatical rules and phrasing, making them less suitable for formal writing. They might be considered \"non-standard\" or \"improper\" grammatically speaking, even though they're still recognizable as elements of the English language.\n\nIn a professional setting, using standard grammatical rules and proper phrasing not only ensures clarity and coherence but also conveys respect for the reader and maintains a formal tone. This can include using full words instead of shortened forms, avoiding contractions, and using precise vocabulary.\n\nHowever, it's worth noting that linguistic variations, such as colloquialisms or regional dialects, can be more nuanced. While in formal writing, standard English is typically used, there's a growing recognition of the importance of cultural and linguistic diversity in communication.\n\nIn the context of your training manual, using standard English conventions helps to maintain a professional tone and ensures that the content is accessible to a broad audience.",
